{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790447295,"nonce":"npMoLd3RwnYuCYwDJ7lVrvtEfv0=","title":"Closures capture a loop-mutated local by value (Issue10083 flips with map order)","message":"Issue10083: `var hasFoo = false; for (flag in flags) switch (flag) { case Foo: hasFoo = true; case Bar: tasks.push(() -\u003e eq(true, hasFoo)); }` then runs the tasks after the loop. Haxe closures capture by reference, so every task sees true. rayzor's result depends on map iteration order (hash order varies run to run: 2 of 6 runs pass with one binary), which means the closure sees hasFoo's value at creation. The capture of a variable reassigned after the closure is made needs a shared cell.","files":null}]}